          <Pad Id="FaNHU5LsYuSK9ijrKPUc5N" Bounds="624,1438,401,434" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="After the cliffhanger of the last chapter I want to at least introduce you to the concept of Member Operations which are already an important base to understand in preparation for object-oriented programming.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You already got to know two operations which are enabled by default in every patch and process node: Update and Create. You can use them anytime to specify if a part of your patch should be executed every frame during runtime or just one frame when the patch or process is initialized. &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Operations like Create and Update which belong to an object in VL, in this case to a Process, are called Member Operation. As a user and designer of your own application you've been given the freedom to define as many Member Operations as you like and assign parts of your Process nodes to them. Once an operation is defined inside of the node, it is available on the outside as a trigger and can be used from there. &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this example, we have implemented a Grow and Shrink operation - look into the definition of the ColoredCircle for further details - note that this time the definition of the Process lies on the definitions side of the patch, right where it belongs.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
