          <Pad Id="H3Vy7m80o3zO94WeRfdHDT" Bounds="2801,1534,400,363" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="It would be a good opportunity to draw your attention to the definitions of a VL document. You can imagine this like the front and back side of your patch - the application is the patch itself and the back is where you store your custom data types, processes and operations. Go to your documents definitions by clicking on the &quot;Definitions&quot; entry in the document menu - this is where the ColoredCircle definition should actually be.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;By the way - when you type something in the Node Browser and select the entry &quot;Node&quot; in there, it automatically creates the Process Node with the name AND the definition of the Process on the backside of your document. A nice little handy shortcut!&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Another side note here, as it is often a cause for trouble - when you are making your way though the Help Browser and copy something nice over to your project, it can happen that some of the nodes are red. Besides referencing the needed libraries you should also check, if the help patch contains custom definitions that you must also copy over to your document. ">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="BPSyB8Dd2eiN09KipKUbWU" Bounds="2801,1497,284,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Application vs. Definitions">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="SSIxqoGHawRLsVROETIaRV" Bounds="2801,1961,401,370" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="There it is, it is spoken out. So far it was easily avoidable to confront you with this term, but now there is no way around it anymore: VL is an object-oriented programming language and by defining a custom Process node, we have just implemented our very first custom object.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The Process basically comes to life in the moment in which it is placed in the patch or when the patch is loaded or re-started. With every other instance of a node that you put in the patch you are just creating more objects, each one with their own lifetime including their Create and Update operations. In the next chapter, we will look into how to deal with custom operations which you can define for any Process node.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You might have already heard about the terms Record and Class, which are other types of objects that you can create in VL. These work a bit differently and are used when you need to create, change and destroy instances of a custom data type during the runtime of your application. But that is another story and shall be told another time.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Bq3fnXET1evM77ENRk2Wmt" Bounds="2801,1924,346,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Object-Oriented Programming">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
